A collection of works of high value
1. I had never heard of TASCHEN before, but recently I bought quite a few of their books because they're so strong in both selection and production.
There have been three versions of this work released so far. The largest version, just like the complete collection of Michelangelo’s works, is approximately 50 centimeters in length and 34 centimeters in width; the medium version is about 36 centimeters in length and 23 centimeters in width; and this version is the smallest one, coming in a set of two books accompanied by a book cover.
Personally, I think it’s quite acceptable. Firstly, the price is low, and the more than 700 pages are divided into two books, which makes them easier to read in paperback format. Secondly, this edition is different from the complete collection of Michelangelo’s works, where paintings take up the majority of the content; in this Da Vinci edition, the black-and-white manuscripts make up the bulk of the content. For readers with limited budgets who are not from an art background, this version offers the best value for money when it comes to appreciating both the paintings and the manuscripts.
2. The commentary on the painting is excellent; it includes analysis of composition, sketches, and historical background (e.g., The Last Supper), but I am unsure whether this was due to printing or book size issues, as some shadows were too dark and difficult to see details, which is somewhat disappointing.
3. Compared to Michelangelo’s manuscripts, Leonardo da Vinci’s manuscripts are much richer in content.
The former was already a genius; the latter, with their wide range of interests and high level of achievement, cannot even be described as a genius.
If you only looked at Da Vinci’s paintings in the past, you would have only been able to appreciate half of his genius; but after seeing the manuscripts, there’s simply no words left to describe it.
It's not as good as it seems.
Compared to the power strips I’ve used in the past, the quality of the exterior construction is average. I haven’t opened it to check the interior, but I hope it’s as the seller described. In terms of convenience of use, there’s no indication as to whether each individual unit is actually powered on or not.
The ForRoyal camera strap and DSLR strap are popular all over the world.
This belt is designed scientifically; it hangs on both shoulders and is practical. The camera is fixed and protected with soft materials at the chest, unlike other fast shooters who fix the bottom of the camera with screws, making it easy to take off photography.
